  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will continue to influence the weather across\nnorthern Ohio and northwest Pennsylvania through Thursday as a\ndominant ridge aloft takes a while to shift. Beginning\nWednesday, this high will begin to slowly shift towards the\nMid-Atlantic Coast, resulting in a southwest flow developing\nacross the area. This will allow for increased moisture\nadvection from the Gulf and warmer air pushing north. Highs to\nstart this week will be in the upper 60s to low 70s but are\nexpected to gradually increase through the week to be in the\nupper 70s to low 80s Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs the aforementioned ridge begins to break down and push east\nmidweek, an upper level trough associated with a surface low\nnear the Four Corners region will begin to push east towards the\narea. The northern portion of this trough will move into the\narea late Thursday, ultimately allowing a cold front to sag\nsouth across the area into Friday. Ahead of this system,\nincreased moisture from a persistent southwest flow will allow \nfor the strong front to bring widespread rain to the area. Given\nthe initial onset of showers overnight, not currently expecting\nany thunder. However, given a general slowing in the \nprecipitation onset amongst models, if the main cold front \npushes east on Friday versus Thursday night, the area could see \nsome thunderstorms activity. This trough should push east late \nFriday, allow for a weak high to build across the area Saturday \nbefore another trough brings rain to the area Sunday.",
